  "narrative": "All estimates are already signed to the registered hypothesis direction, so a positive signed estimate supports a negative-direction dependency claim (raw exposed-minus-comparator more negative) and a negative signed estimate opposes it. H6, SMARCA4 loss versus dependency_SMARCA2, is an unambiguous accepted association in this dataset: signed mean_difference +0.784, 95% CI +0.765 to +0.803, n 412 vs 1588. This is both far outside zero and enormous relative to the SMARCA2 outcome distribution, supporting the synthetic-lethal prediction within the supplied data only. H8, MSI-high versus dependency_WRN, is likewise accepted: signed estimate +0.790, CI +0.760 to +0.819, n 130 vs 1870, indicating much stronger WRN dependency in MSI-high lines in this dataset. H7, KEAP1 loss versus dependency_NFE2L2, strongly rejects the registered negative-direction hypothesis. Its signed estimate is -0.796 with CI -0.816 to -0.776; for direction -1 this corresponds to a positive raw exposed-minus-comparator difference, meaning KEAP1-loss lines have weaker rather than stronger NFE2L2 dependency. I rejected the original claim and proposed a reversed-direction claim to characterize this opposite association.\n\nMost other claims are null or underpowered. H1 was rejected because the signed estimate was negative/opposite and the most favorable upper signed value was only +0.012, well below a likely 10% relative threshold for dependency_BRAF. H10-H12 were rejected because favorable upper bounds were small relative to their outcome scales and CIs included substantial opposite values; the point estimates were near zero. H2, H4, H5, and H9 remain unresolved because their intervals cross zero while allowing clinically meaningful positive signed differences in some cases. H3 is the most scientifically useful borderline result: EGFR amplification versus dependency_EGFR has signed estimate +0.0252, close to 10% relative to the outcome scale, but CI lower bound is -0.00025, so it is not credible without validation. I requested validation for H3. Multiple-comparison risk is low for H6-H8 given how far intervals exclude zero, but H3 remains unresolved because it is near zero and was part of a 12-test discovery family.\n\nBecause critical reviewers must consider technical confounding, I registered follow-up comparisons restricted to the dominant Avana CRISPR library and, for H6, to higher screen quality (screen_roc_auc >= 0.95). These do not change the accepted associations unless the effect fails, but they probe whether the huge H6 and H8 effects could partly reflect screen-platform or quality differences. I also registered a research-marker null-style check for dependency_SMARCA2 to detect latent association structure. All conclusions remain dataset-specific associations, not causal or generalizable claims.",
  "research_notes": "Round 1 evidence: accept H6 and H8 as very strong dataset associations. Reject H7 because effect is strongly opposite; reversed claim registered. Reject H1, H10, H11, H12 as no clinically meaningful predicted effect given signed CIs. Keep H2, H4, H5, H9 deferred unresolved; H3 active pending validation. Next analyze priorities: H3 validation if not already done, reversed H7, H6 restricted to Avana, H6 restricted to screen_roc_auc>=0.95, H8 restricted to Avana, marker nulls, and any untested biologically plausible interactions among accepted dependencies (e.g., does SMARCA2/WRN effect persist across lineage_subtype or TP53 status?). Watch for near-zero outcome scales when applying 10% relative clinical significance, especially USP7 and NFE2L2. Do not infer causality; effects are associations in supplied lung-lineage CRISPR dataset. Validation budget: one requested this iteration.",
