  "narrative": "The two due validation items strengthen the current core findings. For H24, KEAP1 loss in high-quality screens versus dependency_NFE2L2, the high-quality discovery signed estimate was +0.790 (95% CI +0.763 to +0.816), and the strict independent validation signed estimate was +0.799 (independent interval +0.759 to +0.839). Because the direction is positive on a negative-valued dependency score, this corresponds to much weaker NFE2L2 dependency in KEAP1-loss lines. The independent interval is extremely far from null and far above any plausible 10% clinical threshold on the NFE2L2 scale, so H24 remains accepted and is closed. This reinforces the broader accepted KEAP1-loss association while remaining limited to the supplied lung-lineage CRISPR dataset.\n\nFor H59, CDKN2A loss versus dependency_MDM2, the discovery signed estimate was +0.117 (95% CI +0.082 to +0.152), and the independent validation signed estimate was +0.116 (independent interval +0.065 to +0.166). For dependency_MDM2, whose absolute scale is roughly near -0.27, the approximate 10% clinical threshold is about 0.027 in signed magnitude. Both discovery and validation lower bounds exceed that threshold, and the intervals are consistent. Since this claim direction is negative on the raw score, the accepted interpretation is that CDKN2A-loss lines have more negative MDM2 dependency scores, i.e. stronger MDM2 dependency. I therefore accept H59, close the main effect, and retain it as one of the main same-dataset associations.\n\nThe supported dataset-level core associations are now: SMARCA4 loss is associated with stronger SMARCA2 dependency; MSI-high is associated with stronger WRN dependency; KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency, with robust support in high-quality and other technical contexts and independent validation of the high-quality subset; TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency, independently validated; and CDKN2A loss is associated with stronger MDM2 dependency, independently validated. These are associations within this dataset, not causal claims and not claims of external generalizability.\n\nThe most informative next unresolved signal is H90, the reversal of H84. RB1 loss was not associated with stronger DHX9 dependency as originally hypothesized; instead, the reversal shows a positive signed raw difference of +0.0348 (95% CI +0.0091 to +0.0605), suggesting weaker DHX9 dependency in RB1-loss lines. The point estimate exceeds the approximate 0.025 DHX9 threshold, but the lower bound is below threshold and uncertainty is nontrivial, so I keep H90 active for same-dataset robustness and likely validation. H86, NF1 loss versus weaker BRAF dependency, remains borderline: +0.0211 with interval -0.0046 to +0.0468, around but not clearly above the approximate 0.025 BRAF threshold. It remains active but lower priority than H90.\n\nSeveral probes produced negative or low-yield results. ARID1A loss did not support the hypothesized stronger SMARCA2 dependency, and SMARCA4 loss did not support a stronger POLR2A dependency in the registered direction. MTAP co-deletion was much smaller than the CDKN2A effect, and marker controls were near-null relative to the accepted large effects, though they do not strictly exclude small clinically meaningful marker associations. I defer most remaining active marker-null, technical robustness, subtype, and RTK probes because they are less informative than completing validation/robustness for the newly emerging RB1-DHX9 reversal and clarifying NF1-BRAF. The next analyses should focus on whether H90 and H86 survive screen-library, Cas9-activity, and screen-quality restrictions, with H90 as the primary validation candidate.",
  "research_notes": "Core supported same-dataset associations (association only, not causal or generalizable): 1) SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency (H6, robust in technical subsets). 2) MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency (H8, robust in technical subsets). 3) KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency (H17 main; H24 high-quality subset independently validated; H23/H30/H32/H45/H54 accepted subsets). 4) TP53 loss -> weaker USP7 dependency (H49 independently validated; marker D control small). 5) CDKN2A loss -> stronger MDM2 dependency (H59 independently validated; TP53 strata accepted; non-Avana accepted but imprecise; MTAP co-deletion much smaller and closed unresolved). Negative/rejected or closed low-yield: ARID1A loss -> stronger SMARCA2 (H83), SMARCA4 loss -> stronger POLR2A (H85), TP53 loss -> clinically meaningful weaker RPL11 (H16), ERBB2/EGFR cross-dependencies mostly low-yield, MTAP -> MDM2 not explaining CDKN2A, marker D controls small. Active unresolved priorities: H90 RB1 loss -> weaker DHX9 (discovery signed +0.0348, CI +0.0091 to +0.0605; point above ~0.025 threshold but lower bound below; validate/robustness needed). H86 NF1 loss -> weaker BRAF (discovery +0.0211, CI -0.0046 to +0.0468; borderline; lower priority). Deferred secondary active claims: marker-null controls, remaining USP7/MDM2 technical robustness, ARID1A/SMARCA4 context, KRAS/ERBB2/EGFR subtype and cross-RTK probes. Next analyze: H90 Avana, high-Cas9, high screen ROC; H86 Avana and high-Cas9. If H90 robustness does not reverse, request independent validation for H90 in appraise. Continue using strict validation intervals and 10% clinical relevance thresholds based on absolute outcome scale."
